Hmm, scaled fill coordinates get messy. Alternative without clip and without scale: draw the overlay to fill only the ellipse area using ctx.filter blur? Not reliable.

Approach: use radial gradient centered at cave center with radius = max(caveRX, caveRY) and soft edges; no clip. The gradient will spill out as a circle, but because alpha goes to 0 at edge, it won't be a hard blob. However a circular gradient on a 315x330 ellipse is nearly circular, fine. When near the cave, it creates a soft dark vignette around the cave area. That avoids the hard edge entirely and simplifies. But when inside the cave, the screen coverage: cave center projected maybe offscreen, radius 330*scale (~330px at scale 1); view 1440x900; player inside at (250,1648) near bottom of cave; camera centers player, cave center 68 above → circle radius 330 covers only central area, leaving most of the screen bright — bad.

Better: when the player is deep inside the cave (player.y > 1378 && player.x < 475), fill the whole screen with darkness, no ellipse clip, because camera is inside the cave. The cave floor terrain presumably covers the visible area anyway. Outside (approaching), use the soft ellipse fade.

So two states:
- `insideCave = player.y > 1378 && player.x < 475` (torch required to get there)
- if insideCave: darkness full-screen with radial hole around player if torch.
- else near cave (dist < 1.55): soft elliptical darkness over cave area, alpha * fade.

