So render at boot; after that skipped. Yet there's a 3s native program block after load. Could be the browser uploading/compositing the canvas (GPU work) asynchronously, blocking the renderer's main thread on swap.

But hiding the canvas after load still stalled. If the upload happens as a queued task after load, hiding after the check won't cancel it. Timing: we hide immediately after fonts loaded (~340ms). The stall starts ~500-600ms. So the upload may already be queued.

So the upload of the 1440x900 canvas with the drawn ground is the issue. At 0.5x dpr it still stalled 2.6s, so not canvas pixels... unless the *source* ground texture upload (4500x2375) dominates, independent of destination. Yes! Drawing a 1440x900 sub-rect of a 4500x2375 canvas may cause the whole 43MB source texture to upload. That matches the skip-ground test (stall 1s) and why dpr didn't help. And TS=0.6 test still had 2.4s (maybe 17MB upload still slow, plus variance).

Solution: **tile the ground canvas**. Each tile ≤512x512 (or 640x640) so uploads are small and only visible tiles are uploaded. Let's implement tiling.

Plan:
- In terrain.js `paintGround(world, rand)`: paint the full ground canvas as now. Then slice it into tiles of size, say, 512 world pixels * TS? We need world coordinates. Simpler: after painting the full canvas at TS scale, create tiles of 512x512 *pixels* (i.e., 409.6 world units) and store them with pixel coordinates and world dest coordinates. But slicing 4500x2375 into ~9x5=45 tiles creates 45 canvases, adding memory ~43MB total plus overhead. Then null the big canvas so peak is 86MB temporarily. GPU upload is only visible tiles (~9-12 tiles ≈ 10MB). The big source canvas is discarded before first draw, so it never uploads. CPU memory peak is high but manageable (86MB). GC can collect the big canvas.

- Alternatively paint the ground in tiles directly, repeating background functions per tile. The functions paint the entire world (base, paths, river, ruins, cave, city, details) using coordinates; we can set up a context with translate(-tx, -ty) and clip to the tile, then call all paint functions. This repeats loops (e.g., 760 grass tufts) for every tile, multiplying generation time by 45. Not acceptable at load.

- Better: paint full canvas once, slice, drop the full canvas. The upload issue is solved. Peak memory ~86MB; acceptable for a game.

- Even better: use `createImageBitmap` on the full canvas to get an ImageBitmap? That still uploads the full image maybe. Tiling is safer.

Implement:
```js
export function paintGround(world, rand) {
  ... paint big canvas c ...
  // Slice into tiles to keep GPU uploads small.
  const TILE = 512;
  const tiles = [];
  for (let ty=0; ty<c.height; ty+=TILE) {
    for (let tx=0; tx<c.width; tx+=TILE) {
      const tw = Math.min(TILE, c.width-tx);
      const th = Math.min(TILE, c.height-ty);
      const tile = document.createElement('canvas');
      tile.width = tw; tile.height = th;
      tile.getContext('2d').drawImage(c, tx, ty, tw, th, 0,0,tw,th);
      tiles.push({ canvas: tile, x: tx / TS, y: ty / TS, w: tw / TS, h: th / TS });
    }
  }
  // release big canvas
  c.width = 0; c.height = 0;
  return tiles;
}
```
