content.text:
As expected, the title is now a flat dark background. It still looks elegant but loses the lush jungle backdrop, which was a big part of the visual impressiveness. We need to restore the backdrop.

Approach: after the boot render (canvas hidden), call `canvas.toDataURL('image/png')` and set it as a background layer on `.title-screen` (data URL) or an `<img>` behind. The canvas is hidden with display:none, but `toDataURL` still works. Then we can also clear the canvas? Not needed.

Potential issue: a 1440x900 PNG data URL ~1-2MB; setting it as a CSS background triggers decode + upload, which could cause a stall similar to the canvas. But static image compositing is typically much faster than a canvas upload; the earlier stall was likely from canvas command buffer pressure, not the image itself. Test it.

Implementation:
```js
try {
  const art = canvas.toDataURL('image/jpeg', 0.86);
  ui.title.style.setProperty('--title-art', `url("${art}")`);
} catch {}
```
Better use JPEG for smaller size; but the title overlay darkens it, and JPEG artifacts are acceptable. PNG would be lossless but ~1.5MB base64; toDataURL PNG on 1440x900 can take ~200ms. Use `canvas.toDataURL('image/jpeg', 0.82)` ~150KB. The title art is dark; JPEG okay. But transparent? No, the canvas is fully painted with the world, so JPEG fine.

CSS:
```
.title-screen {
  background:
    linear-gradient(...),
    linear-gradient(...),
    var(--title-art) center / cover no-repeat,
    #04100b;
}
```
Note: custom property containing a URL with a huge base64 string in inline style is fine.

But we must ensure the canvas is painted before toDataURL. We call render() at boot, then snapshot. Add after `render(); game.titleDirty=false;`:
```js
try {
  const art = canvas.toDataURL('image/jpeg', 0.85);
  ui.title.style.setProperty('--title-art', `url("${art}")`);
  ui.app.classList.add('title-art-ready');
} catch (e) {}
```
And CSS can show the art layer only when ready. Since the variable is set after CSS, the background updates.

However, `document.fonts.status` may become 'loaded' before this snapshot? It all happens synchronously during module eval before load, so yes.

Also, the title screenshot should use the art. On start, the title overlay hides and the canvas displays; the game renders gameplay. Good.

Potential problem: the capture's auto-start click occurs after the image decode; the data URL decode may add another stall. Test the stall and auto-start 5x after implementation.

Let's implement. Also, the `render()` at boot is with the canvas display:none; does drawing to a display:none canvas still work? Yes, canvas rendering is independent of CSS display. toDataURL works. Good.

We may want to paint the title art at a smaller resolution for memory. But toDataURL from the existing canvas is simplest.

Set the JPEG quality high (0.9). The title overlay is dark so artifacts hidden.

Let's add to game.js boot.

